descartes-discourse-2.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps descartes-discourse-2.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "I received instruction from but one master and never knew the diversities of opinion among the learned", "no opinion, however absurd, can be imagined which has not been maintained by some philosopher" and "those whose opinions are repugnant to ours are not barbarians, and many of them use their reason as well as or better than we do" are true. Is S2, "But I had become aware, even so early as during my college life, that no opinion, however absurd and incredible, can be imagined, which has not been maintained by some on of the philosophers; and afterwards in the course of my travels I remarked that all those whose opinions are decidedly repugnant to ours are not in that account barbarians and savages, but on the contrary that many of these nations make an equally good, if not better, use of their reason than we do.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
